    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Macroscopic and microscopic entropy quantities",
        "reference": "items/def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim.md#definition",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "def-td-si-quantities",
          "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
          "def-natural-logarithm",
          "def-finite-sum",
          "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "physical-assumption",
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-finite-microstate-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-natural-logarithm": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-finite-sum": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. The adopted macroscopic entropy is the finite state function $S_c:D_c\\to\\mathbb R$ of [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], with values in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ under [[def-td-si-quantities]]. Its argument is an equilibrium macrostate. The definition does not assign $S_c$ to an arbitrary nonequilibrium configuration or to a microscopic trajectory.\n\nFor a finite microstate model, use a nonempty labelled set $\\Omega_N=\\{\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_N\\}$, $N\\ge1$, and an assigned energy map $E:\\Omega_N\\to\\mathbb R$ with values in joules, as in the planned TD8 interface of page `td-finite-statistical-bridge`. A chosen coarse-graining is a finite label set $\\mathcal M$ and a map $\\pi:\\Omega_N\\to\\mathcal M$. For $m\\in\\mathcal M$, let $M_m=\\{\\omega_i:\\pi(\\omega_i)=m\\}$ and let $N_m$ be the number of indices in this set. Only labels with $N_m\\ge1$ are admitted here. The Boltzmann macrocell entropy of this specified coarse-graining is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm B}(m):=k_{\\mathrm B}\\log N_m.$$\n\nFor a specified probability vector $p=(p_1,\\ldots,p_N)$, where $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{i=1}^N p_i=1$, define its Gibbs–Shannon entropy by\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm G}(p):=-k_{\\mathrm B}\\sum_{i=1}^N p_i\\log p_i,$$\n\nwith the summand $p_i\\log p_i$ defined to be $0$ when $p_i=0$. Both expressions have units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, because their logarithms are dimensionless and $k_{\\mathrm B}$ has those units. The finite sum and logarithm have the meanings of [[def-finite-sum]] and [[def-natural-logarithm]].\n\nFor a density operator $\\rho$ in the declared finite-dimensional sector class of [[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy]], its von Neumann physical entropy is $S_{\\mathrm{vN}}(\\rho)=k_{\\mathrm B}s(\\rho)$, with $s(\\rho)$ the dimensionless entropy defined there. That supplier defines no such quantity for its infinite-dimensional spatial sectors.\n\nThese are distinct definitions on different domains: $S_c$ acts on an equilibrium macrostate, $S_{\\mathrm B}$ on a chosen macrocell, $S_{\\mathrm G}$ on a specified probability vector, and $S_{\\mathrm{vN}}$ on a finite-sector density operator. A numerical equality among them requires an additional, explicit identification of model, coarse-graining, preparation and applicable limit. This item supplies no such identification and adopts no canonical or microcanonical sampling rule.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, SM: Statistical Mechanics, current author-hosted merged notes, Chapter 5 §§5.1–5.3 and Chapter 6 §§6.1–6.2",
              "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/SM%20merged.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics (work in progress, 12 October 2026), §§8.2, 9.2, and 9.5",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, §§6.3–6.4, printed pp. 122–131",
              "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "I. Burmistrov and A. Lunkin, Lecture Notes on Statistical Mechanics (10 January 2023), §§1.1.1–1.1.3, printed pp. 12–14",
              "url": "http://home.itp.ac.ru/~burmi/HSE/Lectures-StatPhys-temp.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Definitions of the adopted macroscopic equilibrium entropy and selected finite-model microscopic entropy quantities; no identity between these quantities is asserted without a separately specified equilibrium bridge."
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-td-empirical-evidence-contract",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Evidence records for irreversible and transport claims",
        "reference": "items/rem-td-empirical-evidence-contract.md#remark",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "**Evidence record.**\n\nAn empirical claim in a later item must identify the source report and exact locator, the material and sample, apparatus and geometry, boundary and control conditions, operational definitions of measured quantities, calibration, sampling procedure, and the source's reported uncertainty and systematic limitations. Preserve the units, resolution, sample size, and selection conditions actually given by the source. If the source omits one of these details, record the omission and limit the claim; do not supply a plausible value or precision.\n\nKeep four records separate: source-reported observations; derived estimates from those observations; model predictions under stated assumptions; and statistical comparisons between estimates and predictions. State the sampling and measurement model and the inference method when a statistical conclusion is made. Agreement supports the specified model-and-apparatus combination; it does not prove a postulate. Evidence belongs in an experiment item or a nonlogical evidence relation unless a theorem actually assumes a qualified empirical result, in which case that qualification must be carried as an empirical premise.\n\nThe source treatments cited here discuss modelled fluctuations and transport. Their illustrative numerical values are not measurements made by this library. No experiment item or observation is supplied by this remark.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, SM: Statistical Mechanics, current author-hosted merged notes, Chapter 5 §§5.3–5.5",
              "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/SM%20merged.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics (work in progress, 12 October 2026), §§8.2, 8.4, and 9.2",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Requirements for any future empirical record used to discuss thermodynamic fluctuations, local equilibrium or transport; this remark contains no experiment, observation or measured value."
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-td-fluctuation-model-hypotheses",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Hypotheses for microscopic fluctuation claims",
        "reference": "items/rem-td-fluctuation-model-hypotheses.md#remark",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim",
          "post-td-macroscopic-second-law"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-macroscopic-second-law": "physical-assumption"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "**Scope.**\n\nThe adopted [[post-td-macroscopic-second-law]] compares the total macroscopic entropies of specified equilibrium endpoints of an admitted isolated process record. It assigns no entropy function to nonequilibrium intermediate configurations and does not assert that every microscopic realization has nonnegative entropy change. The macrostate and finite-model quantities remain distinct as defined in [[def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim]].\n\nA stochastic fluctuation claim needs its own state space or path space, probability law, preparation, driving protocol, time interval, measured or modelled random variable, and averaging convention. A duration $\\tau>0$ is in seconds; a trajectory observable has the units stated by its model (for physical entropy production, $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$; for dimensionless entropy production, no units). If a trajectory variable is called entropy production, define it and state the hypotheses of the relation being used, including the forward and reverse protocols and the support and integrability conditions needed for the stated averages. Positivity of an ensemble mean does not imply positivity in every realization.\n\nFor a precise model, let $X$ be its state space and $\\Gamma$ its specified set of sample paths $\\gamma:[0,\\tau]\\to X$, for a fixed duration $\\tau>0$. An adopted probability measure $P$ on $\\Gamma$ makes an observable $\\Sigma:\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R$ a random variable; its mean $\\mathbb E_P[\\Sigma]$ is used only when $\\Sigma$ is $P$-integrable. A driving protocol is a declared control map $\\lambda:[0,\\tau]\\to\\Lambda$, with its parameter space $\\Lambda$, regularity and endpoint controls specified by the model. Any reverse-protocol comparison needs its own path measure and support conditions.\n\nThe fluctuation–dissipation theorem discussed in the cited treatments relates equilibrium fluctuations to a specified linear response of a coupled model. It is distinct from a trajectory fluctuation relation and does not by itself prove the macroscopic second-law postulate. No universal microscopic fluctuation formula or trajectory-wise entropy inequality is asserted here.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, SM: Statistical Mechanics, current author-hosted merged notes, Chapter 5 §§5.1–5.7",
              "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/SM%20merged.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics (work in progress, 12 October 2026), §§9.2–9.5",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Distinguishes the adopted macroscopic endpoint entropy postulate from stochastic claims about trajectories, ensemble averages and fluctuations; no microscopic fluctuation relation is proved here."
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Scope of a local-equilibrium continuum branch",
        "reference": "items/rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch.md#remark",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "**Scope and objects.**\n\nFor a declared continuum model, let $\\mathcal O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open spatial region with coordinates in metres and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval with coordinates in seconds. The model chooses a local-state parameter set $\\mathcal Z\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ and a field $z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)$ whose component functions have continuous first partial derivatives in the spatial coordinates and time on $\\mathcal O\\times I$. A component temperature field has type $T:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to(0,\\infty)$ and values in kelvins. If the model includes mass density and fluid velocity, their types are $\\rho:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to[0,\\infty)$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$ and $\\mathbf u:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; these fields may also be required to have continuous first partial derivatives. Each model must specify its remaining coordinates and equation of state; $\\mathcal Z$ is not universal.\n\nA **local-equilibrium approximation** is the model assumption that each coarse-grained cell at $(\\mathbf x,t)$ is represented by the equilibrium constitutive state labelled by $z(\\mathbf x,t)$. A mean free path $\\ell>0\\,\\mathrm m$ is the model's typical distance between scattering events; a field-variation length $L>0\\,\\mathrm m$ is the smallest declared spatial scale over which $z$ changes appreciably. A collision time $\\tau_{\\mathrm c}>0\\,\\mathrm s$ is a declared microscopic relaxation time, and a protocol time $\\tau_{\\mathrm m}>0\\,\\mathrm s$ is the timescale of imposed variation. A kinetic local-equilibrium regime may require $\\ell/L\\ll1$ and $\\tau_{\\mathrm c}/\\tau_{\\mathrm m}\\ll1$. These are qualitative regime inequalities, not universal numerical thresholds. In kinetic descriptions the actual distribution may be $f=f_0(z)+\\delta f$; the local-equilibrium distribution alone need not solve the evolution equation.\n\nNonconstant fields $T(\\mathbf x,t)$, $\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)$, or $\\mathbf u(\\mathbf x,t)$ are permitted in this continuum model and do not describe global equilibrium. Initial data, boundary conditions, conservation equations, constitutive assumptions and their empirical scope must be supplied before any time evolution or transport conclusion is claimed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, SM: Statistical Mechanics, current author-hosted merged notes, Chapter 6 §6.1",
              "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/SM%20merged.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics (work in progress, 12 October 2026), §§8.2, 8.4, and 8.8",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "A prospective continuum description of a specified material on a three-dimensional spatial region and time interval; no evolution law or empirical identification is asserted."
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-td-unitary-entropy-invariance",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Unitary invariance of finite-sector quantum entropy",
        "reference": "items/pthm-td-unitary-entropy-invariance.md#statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim",
          "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy",
          "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-declared-fixed-n-model",
          "def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution",
          "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units",
          "pthm-nrqm-many-body-unitary-evolution-preserves-entropy",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-nrqm-many-body-framework-declared-fixed-n-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-nrqm-many-body-unitary-evolution-preserves-entropy": "physical-result",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Adopt exactly the state, observable, autonomous-dynamics and canonical-ensemble assumptions, readings, finite-dimensional sector class, and normalized canonical preparation of [[pthm-nrqm-many-body-unitary-evolution-preserves-entropy]]. The sector $\\mathfrak H_N^\\sigma$ and its self-adjoint energy operator $H_N^\\sigma$ are those of [[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-declared-fixed-n-model]]. Model time $t$ is in seconds, $H_N^\\sigma$ is in joules, and $\\hbar=h/(2\\pi)$ is the reduced Planck constant in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, as in [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]]. The evolution definition [[def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution]] gives $U(t)=\\exp(-itH_N^\\sigma/\\hbar)$ and $\\rho(t)=U(t)\\rho(0)U(t)^*$. Both the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice are assumed ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Define $S_{\\mathrm{vN}}(\\rho)=k_{\\mathrm B}s(\\rho)$ as in [[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy]]. Then, for every declared sector preparation $\\rho(0)$ and every $t\\in\\mathbb R$:\n\n1. $S_{\\mathrm{vN}}(\\rho(t))=S_{\\mathrm{vN}}(\\rho(0))$;\n2. the adopted canonical preparation is stationary; and\n3. a preparation different from the canonical one is never mapped to it by this unitary flow.\n\nThese are statements about the exact closed finite-sector model. They do not identify $S_{\\mathrm{vN}}$ with the macroscopic equilibrium entropy $S_c$ of [[def-td-macro-versus-micro-entropy-claim]], derive the canonical preparation, or establish bath relaxation or thermalization.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "J. Binney and D. Skinner, The Physics of Quantum Mechanics, §§6.3–6.4, printed pp. 122–131",
              "url": "https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/JamesBinney/qb.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "I. Burmistrov and A. Lunkin, Lecture Notes on Statistical Mechanics (10 January 2023), §§1.1.1–1.1.3, printed pp. 12–14",
              "url": "http://home.itp.ac.ru/~burmi/HSE/Lectures-StatPhys-temp.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "The exact closed, finite-dimensional sector model and unitary dynamics of the named NRQM supplier, including its adopted canonical preparation and stated choice assumptions; the result identifies no macroscopic entropy, open-system relaxation, or laboratory behaviour.",
        "strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-td-transport-branch",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Scope of transport constitutive laws",
        "reference": "items/rem-td-transport-branch.md#remark",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; prerequisites and independent review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "**Scope.**\n\nUse the fields and local-equilibrium regime of [[rem-td-local-equilibrium-branch]]. A Fourier heat-transport model may postulate\n\n$$\\mathbf j_Q(\\mathbf x,t)=-K(\\mathbf x,t)\\nabla T(\\mathbf x,t),$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf j_Q:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is a continuous heat-energy flux field in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$ and $K:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ is a declared conductivity tensor with units $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. In an isotropic model $K=\\kappa I_3$ with $\\kappa\\ge0$. A diffusion model may separately postulate $\\mathbf j_n=-D\\nabla n$, where number density $n:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to[0,\\infty)$ is in $\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, number flux $\\mathbf j_n:\\mathcal O\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is in $\\mathrm{m^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}$, and $D$ is a declared scalar or tensor in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$.\n\nThese are constitutive choices for specified material, geometry, regime, field regularity and boundary conditions. A collision invariant in a kinetic model is a quantity preserved by each allowed collision. Near-equilibrium linear response, a dilute-gas Boltzmann model, a relaxation-time approximation, and a quantum transport model have different hypotheses and coefficient definitions. The relaxation-time approximation can fail to preserve collision invariants; a coefficient derived in one approximation is not thereby universal. Onsager reciprocity is a relation among linear-response coefficients under a specified microscopic time-reversal and conjugate-force convention; this remark does not assert it without those hypotheses. Microscopic calculations and measurements may determine or test coefficients for a specified model and material, but neither the equilibrium entropy postulates nor dimensional consistency alone determine them.\n\nAny heat equation or transport conclusion additionally needs the relevant conservation balance, sources or work terms, constitutive relation, initial data and boundary conditions. No such evolution equation, Onsager relation, or transport coefficient is proved or adopted by this remark.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics, SM: Statistical Mechanics, current author-hosted merged notes, Chapter 6 §§6.1–6.5",
              "url": "https://essentialgraduatephysics.org/Files/SM%20merged.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics (work in progress, 12 October 2026), §§8.2–8.9",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2025/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Prospective near-equilibrium continuum transport models with declared local fields and material-specific constitutive data; no universal transport coefficient or experimental value is asserted."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
